Subject: [PATCH 0/1] MicroBlaze: a QEMU baseboard, work in progress

This is a work in progress and needs more review than it has had.  I am
sending it now because Jeff may want to try it before the __moddi3 fix
lands, and because the one patch is easier to argue about than a
description of it.  I plan to clean it up once that fix is in.

MicroBlaze gets compile-only testing from GCC, and the reason is mostly
that there is nowhere to run the binaries.  DejaGnu has no board for it;
the nearest thing is baseboards/qemu.exp, which names microblaze-* and
microblazeel-* for QEMU user mode, and whose qemu_load calls "array
size" on local_exec's list return, so it reports pass unconditionally
whatever happens.

The patch adds a board for QEMU *system* emulation, which needs a CPU
and a UART rather than the Linux syscall ABI.  It is the usual *-sim
shape, and deliberately so: libgloss and newlib already supply the crt0,
the linker script, the C library and the syscalls, so the board is
libgloss_include_flags, newlib_include_flags, libgloss_link_flags,
newlib_link_flags and ldscript "", plus the three things QEMU needs that
a simulator does not.  Those three are what want reviewing.

What it has been run on -----------------------

A combined tree of gcc trunk 17.0.0, newlib and libgloss from sourceware
unmodified, and binutils 2.47.50, configured --target=microblaze-elf
--with-newlib --disable-multilib, with stock qemu-system-microblaze on
petalogix-s3adsp1800.  Eight gcc.c-torture/execute tests across all
seven torture levels:

  20010221-1.c, loop-2b.c, memcpy-1.c   8 PASS each
  990208-1.c, printf-1.c                7 PASS each
  arith-rand-ll.c                       8 FAIL
  pr50865.c                             7 FAIL
  pr28982b.c                            UNSUPPORTED

53 compiles, no excess errors.  Both failures are __moddi3 in trunk's
libgcc rather than anything in the board: pr50865.c hangs and
arith-rand-ll.c aborts, and both pass when a fixed __moddi3 is linked
ahead of libgcc.  pr50865.c has been in GCC's testsuite since 2011, and
it has been failing on MicroBlaze without anyone being able to see it.
That is the case for having a board at all, and the __moddi3 fix is on
gcc-patches.

The table was taken with this board before I adapted it to find the
runner on PATH and take libxil.a from MB_LIBXIL rather than from beside
itself; the generated command lines are the same either way.  printf-1.c
and pr50865.c were re-run on the patch exactly as sent, 7 passes and 7
failures respectively, which agrees.

Eight tests is not a testsuite.  I picked them to exercise stdio,
malloc, a large stack frame and long long arithmetic, plus the known
failure.  A full run is hours and I have not done one.

Three things I would like opinions on
------------------------------------

1.  Where the runner belongs.  QEMU cannot return a guest exit status
    and libgloss's _exit is "bri 0", so something has to stop QEMU once
    the test has reported.  The board sets needs_status_wrapper and
    reads the status from testglue.c's "*** EXIT code N" line, as
    arm-sim.exp does, and contrib/microblaze-qemu-run watches for that
    line and kills QEMU. I put it in contrib because that is where
    sum2junit.sh lives and because every board DejaGnu ships expects its
    runner on PATH rather than inside the tree.  The board takes it from
    PATH under that name or from $SIM, after mmixware-sim.exp.  libexec/
    or somewhere else may be the better answer.

2.  Whether microblaze-qemu-libxil.c belongs here at all.  GCC's
    MicroBlaze LIB_SPEC links "-lgloss -lxil -lc -lm", and libgloss and
    newlib supply three of those.  The fourth is the board's own
    library, which nothing upstream builds, because in Xilinx's world it
    comes from the tools' board support package; libgloss's write() and
    read() are loops over an outbyte() and inbyte() that are declared
    and never defined.  So some forty lines of UART driver are needed
    before a MicroBlaze bare-metal link completes, and I have put them
    beside testglue.c on the grounds that it is the same kind of file.
    The alternative is that it belongs in libgloss and this patch should
    wait for that.

3.  The environment variables.  MB_QEMU, MB_TIMEOUT, MB_LIBXIL and
    MB_NO_RELAX.  The first two match what other boards do with $SIM and
    friends, but four is more than I would like, and MB_LIBXIL exists
    only because of item 2.

One thing that is not optional yet ----------------------------------

MB_NO_RELAX=-Wl,--no-relax is needed on current binutils, and the board
is useless without it.  testglue.c defines __wrap_exit and calls exit,
so the one hash entry sits in two elf_sym_hashes slots;
microblaze_elf_relax_section walks that array by index and subtracts the
relaxation fixup once per slot, so __wrap_exit resolves before its own
first instruction.  No status line is written and every execution test
reports 2, which looks like the board's fault.  GCC's LINK_SPEC passes
-relax unconditionally.

RISC-V had the same defect and fixed it as PR ld/22756; the guard is
also in elf32-cr16.c, elf32-crx.c, elf32-ft32.c and elfnn-loongarch.c.
I have a patch for MicroBlaze and will send it to binutils separately.
Until it lands, --no-relax is the workaround, and while it is in force
the suite does not exercise relaxation at all.

